Ray <bear@sonic.net> wrote:
>
> In a public-repository model, doesn't there still have to be someone 
> who says yes or no to individual proposed changes and patches?  

It's called "public" because everybody can get the development version
and see what's going and make patches based on the dev code and not on
old release code.

It's not like a wiki where usually everybody can change anything.

> What 
> stops someone from uploading a "game balance fix" that puts a wand of
> wishing with 12 charges in the starting room  ('Coz, obviously, the 
> game is impossible without at least that, right)?   Or a "save and 
> restore fix" that "modernizes" the game save and restore capabilities 
> to allow players to save at any point and restore from any save ('Coz, 
> duh, that's what save and restore does, why hadn't you guys fixed this
> obvious bug already)?

Of course usually only a selected few have commit rights and those
rights are normally given to people that have proven themselves to
behave and to be a valuable addition to the developer team.

> There's a difference between open development and *wide* open 
> development, 

Just because somebody submits a patch you as one of the core
developers are not obligated to add it to your project.

But we have seen in the past that people submit much better patches if
they are able to participate in the development of the project.

It is also much less work for the core developers if the patches they
get apply cleanly to the dev version and they don't have to clean up
the patches themselves.

Bugs also get fixed more easily, for the same reasons.

> and it requires rules and politics.  And not every bad 

Not necessarily. Nowadays there are distributed code repository
systems like git or mercurial.

With those anybody has locally a complete repository and you can
change and patch as much as you like. If somebody else wants to add
your changes to his own repository he just "pulls" them from your
public repository.

Of course somebody must be responsible for finally doing a release,
but technically every developer is on the same level.

> idea will be as obviously bad as the two I mentioned above.  There 
> are points where experienced devs who understand the game can 
> disagree with good arguments on both (or all) sides, and some kind 
> of procedure has to exist for the opinions of one (or some) to be 
> picked over the opinions of others.  

It depends on the project team how disagreements are resolved. There
are always discussions but at the end of the day different teams have
different approaches.

But there have been disagreements in the past that lead to forks, for
example OpenBSD from FreeBSD because the developers were at odds with
each other.

